new_answer:
In Angular a directive hook is just a property on the class that the framework looks for by name. Function shorthand lets me assign an arrow function directly to that property instead of writing a full method. For example, instead of

ngOnInit() { this.load(); }

I can write

ngOnInit = () => { this.load(); };

Because arrow functions capture the lexical this, the hook still sees the component instance without the need for explicit binding. The syntax is shorter, reduces boilerplate, and is handy when I want to inline a small piece of logic or when I’m using a functional style. The trade‑off is that the property is created at runtime rather than being part of the prototype, which can slightly increase memory usage if many instances are created. It also makes the class less compatible with tools that expect standard method definitions, such as some lint rules or certain debugging tools that rely on method names. Overall, I use the shorthand for concise, context‑safe hooks, but I switch to the traditional method syntax for more complex lifecycle logic or when I need to preserve the prototype chain for inheritance.
